    "text_delta": "ide regarding my interviews and areas for growth.\"*\n\n#### Step 2: Request the Scoring Matrix / Public Records (If applicable)\nIn most jurisdictions, municipal hiring processes are subject to merit-based hiring regulations and **Freedom of Information (FOI) / Public Records Act** requests:\n* You generally have the right to request the hiring rubric, evaluation criteria, and interview scoring notes (often with candidate names redacted).\n* This provides objective data on whether the panel followed a standardized scoring framework or if scoring was arbitrary.\n\n#### Step 3: Assess the Culture and History\nResearch the municipality’s leadership team and council:\n* How many department directors are women?\n* Does the municipality have formal Equity, Diversity, and Inclusion (EDI) policies that were violated by assembling a single-gender panel?\n* If there is an entrenched \"boys' club\" dynamic, consider whether working under that leadership would have been supportive of your long-term success.\n\n#### Step 4: Consult a Professional or Legal Advisor (Confidential)\nIf you obtain records that show irregularities (e.g., the minimum qualifications for the posting were altered, scores were clearly manipulated, or standard merit procedures were bypassed), you can consult an employment attorney or your jurisdiction’s human rights commission/ombudsman before deciding whether to lodge a formal complaint.\n\n---\n\n### Final Takeaway\nYou should not dismiss your instincts—the panel composition and qualification disparity warrant scrutiny. However, **hold off on raising the panel's gender composition explicitly** until you have received their feedback and reviewed the objective documentation of the selection process.\n",
