    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "Your frustration and skepticism are completely understandable. On paper, you hold advanced technical credentials, professional licensing, and substantial municipal experience, whereas the successful candidate appears to have fewer traditional planning credentials and an interview panel composed entirely of men. \n\nHere is an objective breakdown of what an all-male panel might signify, how municipal director-level hiring typically works, and how to approach next steps strategically.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Is it a sign of anything?\n\n#### A. Affinity and Unconscious Bias\nAn all-male panel evaluating candidates is a recognized risk factor for **affinity bias** (the tendency to favor candidates who feel familiar, relatable, or \"like us\") and **in-group favoritism**. Even when panel members do not intend to discriminate, unconscious perceptions of \"gravitas,\" \"executive presence,\" or \"culture fit\" frequently track demographic similarities. Best practices in public-sector human resources strongly recommend diverse panels precisely to guard against this.\n\n#### B. Leadership vs. Technical Focus at the Director Level\nAt the Director level in municipal government, selection criteria often shift significantly from technical planning competence (which you clearly have) toward **political navigation, council management, and community conflict resolution**. \n* If the municipality is currently facing contentious developments, rezoning disputes, or community backlash, the panel may have prioritized \"extensive experience in public consultation\" and soft-skills diplomacy over formal planning qualifications.\n* Geography degrees and engagement backgrounds are not uncommon in municipal administration, particularly if the role is viewed internally as political/managerial rather than technical.\n\n#### C. Structural Representation in Local Government\nIn many mid-sized municipalities, senior executive ranks (e.g., CAO/City Manager, City Engineer, Director of Public Works, Legal Counsel) still skew male. If the hiring committee consisted of the CAO and peer department heads or council representatives, the composition may reflect existing municipal demographics rather than a deliberate exclusion of women.\n\n**Summary:** The all-male panel is a legitimate red flag regarding the municipality’s HR maturity and DEI standards, but by itself, it is not definitive proof of intentional bias.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Should you \"make something of it\"?\n\nHow you handle this depends on your primary goal: **getting actionable feedback and protecting your career options**, or **holding the municipality accountable**.\n\n#### Tactical Considerations:\n* **Accusations of bias without documented proof:** Pointing out the all-male panel directly to the hiring committee or HR at this stage is very likely to put them on the defensive. It risks having your inquiry dismissed as \"sour grapes\" and could damage your reputation in the regional municipal planning network.\n* **The 2-week silence:** In municipal government, a two-week delay for formal feedback is common. Selection reports, council notifications, and contract formalizations often take weeks to resolve before HR can issue post-interview debriefs.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Recommended Next Steps\n\n#### Step 1: Follow Up Professionally on the Written Feedback\nSend a polite, neutral follow-up regarding your request for feedback. Frame it around your professional development:\n\n> *\"I am following up on my note from two weeks ago regarding the Director of Planning recruitment. As I continue my professional development and look ahead to future leadership opportunities, I would greatly value any constructive feedback the panel or HR could provide regarding my interviews and areas for growth.\"*\n\n#### Step 2: Request the Scoring Matrix / Public Records (If applicable)\nIn most jurisdictions, municipal hiring processes are subject to merit-based hiring regulations and **Freedom of Information (FOI) / Public Records Act** requests:\n* You generally have the right to request the hiring rubric, evaluation criteria, and interview scoring notes (often with candidate names redacted).\n* This provides objective data on whether the panel followed a standardized scoring framework or if scoring was arbitrary.\n\n#### Step 3: Assess the Culture and History\nResearch the municipality’s leadership team and council:\n* How many department directors are women?\n* Does the municipality have formal Equity, Diversity, and Inclusion (EDI) policies that were violated by assembling a single-gender panel?\n* If there is an entrenched \"boys' club\" dynamic, consider whether working under that leadership would have been supportive of your long-term success.\n\n#### Step 4: Consult a Professional or Legal Advisor (Confidential)\nIf you obtain records that show irregularities (e.g., the minimum qualifications for the posting were altered, scores were clearly manipulated, or standard merit procedures were bypassed), you can consult an employment attorney or your jurisdiction’s human rights commission/ombudsman before deciding whether to lodge a formal complaint.\n\n---\n\n### Final Takeaway\nYou should not dismiss your instincts—the panel composition and qualification disparity warrant scrutiny. However, **hold off on raising the panel's gender composition explicitly** until you have received their feedback and reviewed the objective documentation of the selection process.\n",
